/logs/agent/trajectory.json:200:      "message": "Analysis: The WAL file was removed/truncated by our PRAGMA wal_checkpoint. SQLite still reports WAL mode and only 5 rows. We need try to recover the original WAL from the overlay lower layer or use other forensic/FS methods before reconstructing data. I will inspect the full mount info, look for accessible overlay lower/upper paths, and inspect the database contents for clues.\nPlan: Print full mountinfo to identify overlay lower/upper directories, test whether those host paths are visible, search for WAL remnants or copies, and inspect main.db strings/hexdump fully for any leftover hidden data.",
/logs/agent/trajectory.json:201:      "reasoning_content": "We need solve task. We accidentally removed WAL by checkpoint? We need recover all 11 records. Initial WAL present 16512 bytes. PRAGMA wal_checkpoint apparently reset/removed WAL and lost WAL changes? It returned 0|0|0 weird because maybe it ignored corrupted WAL? But now WAL gone. Need reconstruct WAL or find original WAL. We may need use environment clues. The task says \"WAL file appears to be corrupted or encrypted. SQLite may only show base data (5 records) instead of all 11 records.\" There was WAL file before; our checkpoint removed it maybe without reading due corrupted header? Need fix WAL. But we deleted it. We need recover from perhaps hidden in overlay lowerdir or container snapshot. We can inspect mount info full, lowerdir maybe accessible from /proc? We are root in container, may have /data/containerd? Let's see. Need find original file in lower layers maybe accessible if mount namespace has path? likely /data/containerd paths not mounted? But maybe in root overlay? We can list /data. Need not panic; WAL may be recoverable from deleted? It wasn't deleted before by checkpoint? The find after ls says only main.db. Since WAL removed. But perhaps we can recover via SQLite journal? no.\n\nNeed understand SQLite WAL format. It may be corrupted/encrypted. We can attempt reconstruct rows. Base DB has 5 rows, expected 11. Maybe WAL contains 6 rows with encrypted payload but can decrypt? Need locate original WAL content maybe in cache? Our shell commands have no history? We can inspect command output? Not.\n\nLet's examine tasks. Usually in these benchmark tasks, there is a database in WAL mode with WAL file. The WAL file is intentionally corrupted/encrypted, but sqlite only sees base. Need fix WAL. The WAL file may be hidden because PRAGMA checkpoint ignored/truncated; maybe we should not have run. But perhaps need restore from original image. Since container overlay, initial files from image lower layer maybe accessible via lowerdir; modifications (checkpoint removal) only in upperdir. Need find lower layer path. mountinfo lowerdir path appears from host not accessible maybe but we can check. It says lowerdir=/data/containerd/... but /data likely not in container? Let's test. Also overlay filesystem root mount has lowerdir, maybe /proc/1/root/data? Hmm. We need find original WAL in lowerdir or deleted lower file? The upper dir hides it? Actually when PRAGMA checkpoint removes WAL by unlink/truncate in upper layer? For a file copied up from lower layer, deleting it in upper layer creates whiteout in upper layer; but lower file still in overlay lower. Since /app is overlay; list won't show whiteouted lower file if whiteout. If we can access lower layer via host? Could maybe access through /proc/1/root/data? The root is overlay mount. But maybe /data path from host is not mounted inside namespace. However root can maybe access /proc/1/root/mnt? no.\n\nAlternative: Reconstruct WAL from checkpoint? The checkpoint may have written WAL frames into main.db? But it returned 0|0|0 and row count remains 5. It likely ignored WAL and truncated/removed, no write. But main.db still size 8192 and page count 2? Let's inspect. Base schema page maybe page1. Table data in page 2? 8192 two pages. It might have the 5 rows.\n\nMaybe after WAL removed, we can recreate valid WAL by writing the missing records directly to main.db (journal mode) and then create recovered.json. The expected all 11 records need data. Need know missing names/values. Maybe base data suggests item1..item11 with values something. Task says \"should have format: [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"item1\", \"value\": X}, ...]\". It may be a clue: names are item1 etc. But base names apple/banana... not item1. Actually output format example [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"item1\", \"value\": X}, {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"item2\", \"value\": Y}]. The actual database maybe names are item1? But base names are apple etc. Could be just example. We need extract all data including WAL changes. Maybe missing records are \"item6\"...\"item11\" or \"fig/grapes...\"? Need recover from WAL before deletion. But we can reconstruct if enough clues.\n\nLet's inspect main.db pages and maybe deleted page data? There might be old pages with row data from previous versions in free pages? Need hexdump full database. Also check file timestamps maybe original. We can inspect sqlite page content. Use strings. Also maybe WAL file is cached in filesystem page cache? lsof +L1 none. But maybe can recover via debugfs? Overlay whiteout? Let's inspect /proc/self/mountinfo full and try find lowerdir paths.",
/logs/agent/trajectory.json:223:            "keystrokes": "find / -name 'main.db-wal' -o -name '*.db-wal' 2>/dev/null\n",
