/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:727:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:125:            "content": "New Terminal Output:\n\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# sqlite3 /app/main.db '.schema'\nCREATE TABLE items (\n        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n        name TEXT,\n        value INTEGER\n    );\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# sqlite3 /app/main.db 'SELECT count(*) FROM sqlite_master; SELECT name, sql FROM sqlite_master;'\n1\nitems|CREATE TABLE items (\n        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n        name TEXT,\n        value INTEGER\n    )\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# sqlite3 /app/main.db 'SELECT count(*) FROM items; SELECT * FROM items ORDER BY id;'\n5\n1|apple|100\n2|banana|200\n3|cherry|300\n4|date|400\n5|elderberry|500\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# sqlite3 /app/main.db 'PRAGMA wal_checkpoint;'\n0|0|0\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# xxd -g1 -l 256 /app/main.db\n00000000: 53 51 4c 69 74 65 20 66 6f 72 6d 61 74 20 33 00  SQLite format 3.\n00000010: 10 00 02 02 00 40 20 20 00 00 00 03 00 00 00 02  .....@  ........\n00000020: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 03 00 00 00 04  ................\n00000030: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00  ................\n00000040: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n00000050: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 03  ................\n00000060: 00 2e 6e ba 0d 00 00 00 01 0f 84 00 0f 84 00 00  ..n.............\n00000070: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n00000080: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n00000090: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n000000a0: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n000000b0: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n000000c0: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n000000d0: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n000000e0: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\n000000f0: 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ................\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# xxd -g1 -l 256 /app/main.db-wal\nxxd: /app/main.db-wal: No such file or directory\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app#\n"

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:728:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:139:      "message": "Analysis: The sqlite3 schema and base table are present, but only 5 rows are visible. The WAL file was present initially, but after running PRAGMA wal_checkpoint the file no longer appears. We need inspect whether the WAL was removed/truncated/renamed, and look for any recoverable copies or deleted file handles.\nPlan: List /app again, search filesystem for database/WAL-related files, check for deleted open files, and inspect mount info for possible overlay lower layer visibility. Also query the db again to confirm row count after the checkpoint.",

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:729:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:140:      "reasoning_content": "We need respond with JSON commands. We discovered after PRAGMA wal_checkpoint maybe WAL deleted? Actually output says no such file. PRAGMA checkpoint caused sqlite to checkpoint and delete/truncate WAL? Wait task says WAL corrupted or encrypted; normal sqlite only sees 5, checkpoint returns 0|0|0 because maybe it ignored WAL? But now file gone. Uh oh. Did the checkpoint delete the WAL? It says xxd /app/main.db-wal no such. Let's inspect. Maybe SQLite detected corruption and ignored WAL? It could have deleted WAL file on checkpoint? Wait the WAL file existed before. sqlite3 normal query read only 5, not checkpoint? Running PRAGMA wal_checkpoint on a WAL-mode db might attempt to checkpoint frames; if WAL ignored due to bad header maybe maybe deletes? It seems file now absent. Need recover all 11. Did we destroy the WAL by running checkpoint? We need salvage from filesystem? The WAL might have been moved? Let's check. It might still be at /app/main.db-wal? No. Could be in memory? Let's look. But task has base and WAL. We need recover all 11. We may have just made worse by checkpoint deleting corrupt WAL. But perhaps file is renamed due to zero-length? Let's inspect dir. If gone, maybe data still in base? base only 5. But maybe after checkpoint, the WAL changes merged? It returned 0|0|0 and base maybe still 5? Let's query. Also inspect maybe .wal? If deleted, task may be impossible, but perhaps there is backup hidden or original can be re-created? Need solve.\n\nLet's run ls, find, maybe df, open file descriptors? The command process exited so fd gone. Maybe there is a snapshot? Maybe in /tmp? Need inspect. But before I worried, maybe WAL file gets moved by shell to /app/main.db-wal? no. Maybe PRAGMA wal_checkpoint on a corrupted WAL truncated the WAL to zero, and then on process exit it removes it. If file removed, no WAL. But can still recover? Maybe data not fully in WAL? Hmm.\n\nNeed analyze what happened. The terminal shows after PRAGMA wal_checkpoint 0|0|0; then xxd no such file. Maybe checkpoint with WAL unavailable? It didn't merge 11 records. The data 6-11 may still be in the deleted WAL's content in memory? The sqlite3 process ended, so no. But the task may rely on base and WAL; our commands have destroyed it. Need maybe use recovery from page filesystem? Not possible normally. But maybe /app/main.db-wal file was not deleted but file name returned no because current directory changed? We did `sqlite3 /app/main.db 'PRAGMA wal_checkpoint;'`, after command maybe sqlite created /app/main.db-shm? But no. Let's list. If gone, maybe there is a fallback original in /tmp? Let's inspect `ls -la /app /tmp /root`. Also maybe sqlite WAL files are recoverable by ext4 undelete? We're in docker, overlayfs. Could be recover from deleted inode? Need be careful. But maybe not too bad because task designers expected us not to run checkpoint. But terminal environment persistent; maybe can still solve from available artifacts? The deleted WAL may exist in command output? No.\n\nLet's explore. The WAL header is 32 bytes; file size 16512. There are likely 32-byte header plus frames 24+pagesize (4096) maybe 4 frames = 32+4*4116=16496, plus leftover? 16512 = 32 + 4*4120 = 16512 if frame size 4120? Actually SQLite WAL frame = 24-byte header + page size (4096) = 4120. 4 frames = 16480 + 32 = 16512. Yes 4 WAL frames. If corruption/encryption, maybe WAL pages are XORed? The WAL file was deleted by checkpoint due to invalid salt? Need recover deleted file or reconstruct from other info? Maybe the WAL is encrypted using SQLCipher? But no, base is normal SQLite. Maybe \"corrupted or encrypted\" means WAL file content is reversed bytes or XOR? Need inspect but now gone. Could create backup if we had. Too late.\n\nMaybe check filesystem layers: Docker overlayfs may have lower dir with original /app/main.db-wal still accessible? We can inspect mount, overlay lowerdir path maybe. We are root in container but not host. `/proc/self/mountinfo` might show overlay lowerdirs inaccessible. But maybe can access `/proc/1/root/app/main.db-wal`? No same. Deleted file in overlay upper layer not visible. Could recover from /proc? No.\n\nMaybe the WAL deletion is not permanent because sqlite created a zero-length journal in current working dir? Let's inspect.\n\nLet's not panic, run commands. Need perhaps issue `ls -la`, `find / -name '*main.db*' 2>/dev/null`, `lsof +L1`, `grep -a` etc.",

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:730:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:154:            "keystrokes": "find / -xdev \\( -name 'main.db*' -o -name '*.db-wal' -o -name '*.db-shm' \\) -print 2>/dev/null\n",

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:731:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:170:            "keystrokes": "sqlite3 /app/main.db 'PRAGMA journal_mode; SELECT count(*) FROM items;'\n",

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:732:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:186:            "content": "New Terminal Output:\n\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# ls -la /app\ntotal 20\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 root root 4096 Sep 19 23:32 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 root root 4096 Sep 19 23:32 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 8192 Oct 24  2025 main.db\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# find / -xdev \\( -name 'main.db*' -o -name '*.db-wal' -o -name '*.db-shm' \\) -print 2>/dev/null\nlsof +L1 2>/dev/null || true\nsqlite3 /app/main.db 'PRAGMA journal_mode; SELECT count(*) FROM items;'\ncat /proc/self/mountinfo | grep -E '/app|overlay' | head -20\n/app/main.db\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# lsof +L1 2>/dev/null || true\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# sqlite3 /app/main.db 'PRAGMA journal_mode; SELECT count(*) FROM items;'\nwal\n5\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app# cat /proc/self/mountinfo | grep -E '/app|overlay' | head -20\n824 678 0:68 / / rw,relatime - overlay overlay rw,lowerdir=/data/containerd/io.containerd.snapshotter.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/106375/fs:/data/containerd/io.conta\ninerd.snapshotter.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/105828/fs:/data/containerd/io.containerd.snapshotter.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/105827/fs:/data/containerd/io.containerd.sn\napshotter.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/105826/fs:/data/containerd/io.containerd.snapshotter.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/105825/fs:/data/containerd/io.containerd.snapshotte\nr.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/105086/fs,upperdir=/data/containerd/io.containerd.snapshotter.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/106376/fs,workdir=/data/containerd/io.containerd.s\nnapshotter.v1.overlayfs/snapshots/106376/work,index=off\nroot@ce2e7a678869:/app#\n"

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:733:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:200:      "message": "Analysis: The WAL file was removed/truncated by our PRAGMA wal_checkpoint. SQLite still reports WAL mode and only 5 rows. We need try to recover the original WAL from the overlay lower layer or use other forensic/FS methods before reconstructing data. I will inspect the full mount info, look for accessible overlay lower/upper paths, and inspect the database contents for clues.\nPlan: Print full mountinfo to identify overlay lower/upper directories, test whether those host paths are visible, search for WAL remnants or copies, and inspect main.db strings/hexdump fully for any leftover hidden data.",

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:734:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:201:      "reasoning_content": "We need solve task. We accidentally removed WAL by checkpoint? We need recover all 11 records. Initial WAL present 16512 bytes. PRAGMA wal_checkpoint apparently reset/removed WAL and lost WAL changes? It returned 0|0|0 weird because maybe it ignored corrupted WAL? But now WAL gone. Need reconstruct WAL or find original WAL. We may need use environment clues. The task says \"WAL file appears to be corrupted or encrypted. SQLite may only show base data (5 records) instead of all 11 records.\" There was WAL file before; our checkpoint removed it maybe without reading due corrupted header? Need fix WAL. But we deleted it. We need recover from perhaps hidden in overlay lowerdir or container snapshot. We can inspect mount info full, lowerdir maybe accessible from /proc? We are root in container, may have /data/containerd? Let's see. Need find original file in lower layers maybe accessible if mount namespace has path? likely /data/containerd paths not mounted? But maybe in root overlay? We can list /data. Need not panic; WAL may be recoverable from deleted? It wasn't deleted before by checkpoint? The find after ls says only main.db. Since WAL removed. But perhaps we can recover via SQLite journal? no.\n\nNeed understand SQLite WAL format. It may be corrupted/encrypted. We can attempt reconstruct rows. Base DB has 5 rows, expected 11. Maybe WAL contains 6 rows with encrypted payload but can decrypt? Need locate original WAL content maybe in cache? Our shell commands have no history? We can inspect command output? Not.\n\nLet's examine tasks. Usually in these benchmark tasks, there is a database in WAL mode with WAL file. The WAL file is intentionally corrupted/encrypted, but sqlite only sees base. Need fix WAL. The WAL file may be hidden because PRAGMA checkpoint ignored/truncated; maybe we should not have run. But perhaps need restore from original image. Since container overlay, initial files from image lower layer maybe accessible via lowerdir; modifications (checkpoint removal) only in upperdir. Need find lower layer path. mountinfo lowerdir path appears from host not accessible maybe but we can check. It says lowerdir=/data/containerd/... but /data likely not in container? Let's test. Also overlay filesystem root mount has lowerdir, maybe /proc/1/root/data? Hmm. We need find original WAL in lowerdir or deleted lower file? The upper dir hides it? Actually when PRAGMA checkpoint removes WAL by unlink/truncate in upper layer? For a file copied up from lower layer, deleting it in upper layer creates whiteout in upper layer; but lower file still in overlay lower. Since /app is overlay; list won't show whiteouted lower file if whiteout. If we can access lower layer via host? Could maybe access through /proc/1/root/data? The root is overlay mount. But maybe /data path from host is not mounted inside namespace. However root can maybe access /proc/1/root/mnt? no.\n\nAlternative: Reconstruct WAL from checkpoint? The checkpoint may have written WAL frames into main.db? But it returned 0|0|0 and row count remains 5. It likely ignored WAL and truncated/removed, no write. But main.db still size 8192 and page count 2? Let's inspect. Base schema page maybe page1. Table data in page 2? 8192 two pages. It might have the 5 rows.\n\nMaybe after WAL removed, we can recreate valid WAL by writing the missing records directly to main.db (journal mode) and then create recovered.json. The expected all 11 records need data. Need know missing names/values. Maybe base data suggests item1..item11 with values something. Task says \"should have format: [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"item1\", \"value\": X}, ...]\". It may be a clue: names are item1 etc. But base names apple/banana... not item1. Actually output format example [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"item1\", \"value\": X}, {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"item2\", \"value\": Y}]. The actual database maybe names are item1? But base names are apple etc. Could be just example. We need extract all data including WAL changes. Maybe missing records are \"item6\"...\"item11\" or \"fig/grapes...\"? Need recover from WAL before deletion. But we can reconstruct if enough clues.\n\nLet's inspect main.db pages and maybe deleted page data? There might be old pages with row data from previous versions in free pages? Need hexdump full database. Also check file timestamps maybe original. We can inspect sqlite page content. Use strings. Also maybe WAL file is cached in filesystem page cache? lsof +L1 none. But maybe can recover via debugfs? Overlay whiteout? Let's inspect /proc/self/mountinfo full and try find lowerdir paths.",

/logs/agent/terminus_2.pane:735:/logs/agent/trajectory.json:223:            "keystrokes": "find / -name 'main.db-wal' -o -name '*.db-wal' 2>/dev/null\n",

